window.CHAPTERS=[{id:"1",title:"Nature and Significance of Management",focus:"How managers turn shared effort into results by balancing goals, resources and people.",headline:"Management gets people moving in one direction.",takeaways:["Management is a process: planning, organising, staffing, directing and controlling connect rather than happen in isolation.","Effectiveness means achieving the target; efficiency means achieving it with sensible use of resources. Strong management needs both.","Coordination aligns departments and people around shared objectives. It is built into every management function."],flow:[["Plan","Choose goals and action"],["Organise","Group tasks and authority"],["Staff","Put the right people in roles"],["Direct","Guide, motivate, communicate"],["Control","Measure, compare, correct"]],sections:[{title:"What management means",lead:"Management is a continuous process of working with and through people to achieve organisational objectives effectively and efficiently.",items:[["A process","A series of related functions, not one isolated act."],["Goal directed","Effort is coordinated toward stated organisational objectives."],["Through people","Managers obtain results by guiding the work of others."],["Universal","Needed in all organisations: business, public, service, large or small."]]},{title:"Effectiveness and efficiency",lead:"Think of results and resources as two separate tests.",items:[["Effectiveness","Completing the intended task and achieving the target."],["Efficiency","Doing the task correctly with minimum time, cost and waste."],["The exam trap","A target reached through excessive overtime is effective, but may be inefficient. Cost cutting that misses the target is not effective."]]},{title:"Why management matters",lead:"Management makes collective work purposeful and sustainable.",items:[["Achieves group goals","Integrates individual effort into a common direction."],["Increases efficiency","Reduces costs through better use of people and materials."],["Creates a dynamic organisation","Helps the organisation adapt to external change."],["Helps personal objectives","Rewards and development connect personal goals with organisational work."],["Contributes to society","Supports quality goods, employment, innovation and responsible use of resources."]]},{title:"Levels of management",lead:"Authority and the kind of work change as you move down the hierarchy.",items:[["Top level","Board, CEO and senior leaders set objectives, policies and long-range direction."],["Middle level","Department and branch managers interpret plans, organise departments and connect senior leaders with supervisors."],["Operational level","Supervisors and forepersons assign day-to-day work, maintain standards and report results."]]},{title:"The five functions",lead:"A useful sequence is decide → arrange → fill → lead → check.",items:[["Planning","Decide in advance what to do and how to do it."],["Organising","Identify work, group activities, assign duties and establish reporting relationships."],["Staffing","Estimate people needed; recruit, select, place and develop them."],["Directing","Instruct, motivate, lead and communicate so people act."],["Controlling","Set standards, measure performance, compare and correct deviations."]]}],compare:{title:"Quick distinction",heads:["Term","Meaning","Case clue"],rows:[["Effectiveness","Achievement of the stated objective","Target met on time"],["Efficiency","Use of minimum resources for a given result","Less waste, cost or time"],["Coordination","Aligning interdependent efforts","Departments share information"]]},cues:[["Target met but cost rose sharply","Effectiveness"],["Departments protect their own targets and work at cross-purposes","Coordination"],["Supervisors allocate daily duties and monitor workers","Operational management"],["Company changes its approach after customer preferences shift","Dynamic management"]],cards:[["Management","A continuous process of working with and through people to achieve objectives effectively and efficiently."],["Effectiveness","Achieving the stated objective or completing the intended task."],["Efficiency","Doing the task correctly with minimum cost, time or resource waste."],["Coordination","Integrating and harmonising effort toward common goals."],["Planning","Deciding in advance what is to be done and how."],["Organising","Grouping work, assigning duties and establishing authority relationships."],["Staffing","Obtaining, using and maintaining a suitable workforce."],["Directing","Instructing, guiding, motivating and leading employees."],["Controlling","Measuring performance, comparing it with standards and correcting deviations."],["Operational management","Supervising day-to-day work, assigning tasks and maintaining standards."],["Dynamic management","Adapting organisational goals and methods as the external environment changes."]],mcqs:[{q:"A manager reaches the monthly target, but only after costly overtime and excess material use. The result is:",o:["Effective, but not efficient","Efficient, but not effective","Neither effective nor efficient","Both fully effective and efficient"],a:0,x:"The target was achieved, so the work was effective. Excess resource use weakens efficiency."},{q:"Which sequence lists the five functions of management?",o:["Planning, organising, staffing, directing, controlling","Organising, planning, directing, staffing, controlling","Planning, staffing, controlling, organising, directing","Staffing, planning, organising, controlling, directing"],a:0,x:"The functions are planning, organising, staffing, directing and controlling."},{q:"Department heads translate senior management plans into branch targets. They are mainly at the:",o:["Middle level","Operational level","Top level","Informal level"],a:0,x:"Middle managers interpret plans and coordinate their execution."},{q:"Management is universal because it is:",o:["Needed in all kinds of organisations","Identical in every organisation","Performed only by professional managers","Needed only in large businesses"],a:0,x:"The need for management exists across organisations, although practices vary."},{q:"Which statement best distinguishes efficiency from effectiveness?",o:["Efficiency concerns resource use; effectiveness concerns goal achievement","Efficiency concerns goals; effectiveness concerns employee satisfaction","They mean the same thing","Efficiency applies only to production"],a:0,x:"Effectiveness asks whether the objective was achieved; efficiency asks how economically."},{q:"A supervisor makes daily schedules and checks work. This is primarily:",o:["Operational management","Top management","Middle management","External coordination"],a:0,x:"Operational managers supervise day-to-day work."},{q:"Which function first decides what is to be done and how?",o:["Planning","Directing","Controlling","Staffing"],a:0,x:"Planning determines objectives and courses of action in advance."},{q:"Departments duplicate tasks and pursue different targets. The missing force is:",o:["Coordination","Recruitment","Delegation","Standardisation"],a:0,x:"Coordination integrates interdependent effort toward shared objectives."},{q:"Which is a social objective of management?",o:["Supplying quality goods at fair prices","Maximising one executive's bonus","Eliminating every operating cost","Increasing one department's authority"],a:0,x:"Social objectives concern responsible contribution to society and stakeholders."}],subjective:[{m:2,case:"A bakery delivers each wedding cake on the promised date, but it uses twice the planned ingredients and pays heavy rush charges.",q:"Explain whether the bakery is effective and efficient. (2 marks)",key:["It is effective because it meets the delivery objective.","It is inefficient because it uses excessive materials and rush-cost resources."]},{m:2,case:"A regional manager receives an expansion plan and translates it into hiring and production targets for each branch.",q:"Identify the management level and state one role shown. (2 marks)",key:["Middle-level management.","It interprets top-level plans and coordinates execution by departments or branches."]},{m:2,q:"State any two reasons why management is needed in an organisation. (2 marks)",key:["Any two: achieve group goals; increase efficiency; adapt to change; help employees achieve personal objectives; contribute to society."]},{m:2,q:"Distinguish between effectiveness and efficiency. (2 marks)",key:["Effectiveness is achieving the intended objective.","Efficiency is achieving results with minimum resources, time or cost."]},{m:2,q:"What is coordination? Why is it needed when departments specialise? (2 marks)",key:["Coordination integrates and harmonises effort toward common goals.","Specialised departments are interdependent and can otherwise work at cross-purposes."]},{m:2,case:"A supervisor makes daily work schedules, assigns duties and checks safety rules.",q:"Name the management level and one responsibility shown. (2 marks)",key:["Operational or supervisory level.","It supervises day-to-day work, assigns duties, maintains standards or reports results."]},{m:2,q:"Name the five functions of management. (2 marks)",key:["Planning, organising, staffing, directing and controlling."]},{m:2,case:"A company changes its product range after customers begin preferring low-waste packaging.",q:"Which characteristic of management is shown? Explain briefly. (2 marks)",key:["Management is dynamic.","It adapts organisational goals and methods to environmental change."]},{m:3,q:"Explain three ways management helps an organisation. (3 marks)",key:["It achieves group goals by uniting individual effort.","It raises efficiency through better use of resources.","It helps adapt to change, supports employee objectives or contributes to society (one explained)."]},{m:3,q:"Explain the three levels of management and one role of each. (3 marks)",key:["Top: sets objectives, policies and long-term direction.","Middle: interprets plans and coordinates departments.","Operational: supervises daily work and maintains standards."]},{m:3,case:"A firm misses its delivery promise after cutting staff below the level needed. The manager says costs fell, so performance improved.",q:"Assess the claim using effectiveness and efficiency. (3 marks)",key:["Lower costs may indicate efficiency in resource use.","Missing the delivery promise means the firm did not achieve its objective, so it was ineffective.","Good management balances both; cost reduction alone is not success."]},{m:3,q:"Describe any three characteristics of management. (3 marks)",key:["Any three explained: goal-oriented; pervasive; multidimensional; continuous; group activity; dynamic; intangible; process."]},{m:3,case:"Marketing promised a launch date without consulting production; sales staff have no stock information.",q:"Identify the management problem and explain two likely effects. (3 marks)",key:["Poor coordination.","Interdependent departments are not aligned.","Effort may be duplicated, delayed or common objectives missed."]},{m:3,q:"Explain how management of work, people and operations are connected. (3 marks)",key:["Work defines what the organisation must accomplish.","People must be guided and organised to perform it.","Operations transform inputs into goods or services; the three dimensions depend on one another."]},{m:3,case:"A sales manager asks the team to hit a target; another manager checks customer complaints and employee fatigue.",q:"Name two management concerns and identify a clue for each. (3 marks)",key:["Effectiveness: achieving the sales target.","Efficiency or people management: checking resources, service quality or employee conditions.","Management balances results with how work is carried out."]},{m:3,q:"Explain three contributions of coordination to management. (3 marks)",key:["Integrates effort around common objectives.","Links specialised and interdependent departments.","Prevents duplication, conflict or waste; needed at every level (one explained)."]},{m:4,case:"A furniture maker sets a monthly output target, groups production tasks, hires trained carpenters, briefs teams and compares daily output with the plan.",q:"Identify four management functions and match each to an action. (4 marks)",key:["Planning: sets the target.","Organising: groups tasks.","Staffing: hires trained carpenters.","Directing: briefs teams; controlling: compares output with the plan (any four matched correctly)."]},{m:4,q:"Explain why management is described as continuous, pervasive and a group activity. (4 marks)",key:["Continuous: functions recur and overlap as work proceeds.","Pervasive: all organisations and levels need management.","Group activity: people work together toward shared objectives.","Connect the features to coordinated, ongoing effort."]},{m:4,case:"A hospital meets its patient target by rushing consultations; costs fall, but errors and complaints rise. The director proposes a quality review and revised staffing plan.",q:"Evaluate performance and explain how two functions could improve it. (4 marks)",key:["Target volume suggests some effectiveness, but rising errors show quality objectives are missed.","Lower cost alone does not establish efficiency if service quality deteriorates.","Controlling can compare standards with actual results and correct deviations.","Planning and staffing can set realistic targets and provide qualified staff."]},{m:4,q:"Explain the five functions of management as an integrated process. (4 marks)",key:["Planning sets objectives and action.","Organising divides and assigns work; staffing fills roles.","Directing guides and motivates people.","Controlling checks performance and feeds corrections into future plans."]},{m:4,case:"A growing retailer's purchase, sales and delivery teams make independent decisions. Orders are duplicated and customers receive conflicting information.",q:"Explain why coordination becomes more important as organisations grow and give three managerial responses. (4 marks)",key:["Growth increases people, units and interdependencies.","Specialisation can create silos and conflicting priorities.","Set shared goals and clear communication routines.","Coordinate plans and information across functions; monitor service standards."]},{m:4,q:"Describe the three management levels and explain how they connect plans to daily work. (4 marks)",key:["Top managers set long-term objectives and direction.","Middle managers translate strategy into departmental plans.","Operational managers assign and supervise daily work.","Information and feedback connect the levels so action stays aligned."]}]}];
